window.docContents[235].resolve({"/Dependency-Atlas/#PDEAtlas--Dependency-Atlas":{"contents":"The graph collects the definitions and theorems presented in the book.\nAn arrow points from a prerequisite to a statement that uses it. Dashed groups\norganize statements by subject; they are not additional theorems.\nThe graph records the dependencies attached to the statement and proof cards,\nrather than every auxiliary lemma in the Lean source.\n\nFor weak derivatives, the\nuniqueness theorem says that two\nfunctions satisfying the same\ntest-function identity agree almost everywhere.\nThat explains why Sobolev derivatives can be stored as  classes rather\nthan as pointwise functions.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"11. Dependency Atlas","id":"/Dependency-Atlas/#PDEAtlas--Dependency-Atlas"},"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Regularity-and-geometry":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Elliptic Coverage and Open Problems","header":"5.7.1.3. Regularity and geometry","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Regularity-and-geometry"},"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Equilibrium___-representation___-and-coercivity":{"contents":"The prototype is Laplace's equation , whose solutions are called\nharmonic, and harmonic functions are remarkably constrained. They satisfy the\nmean-value property and are real-analytic in the interior. On a bounded domain,\none that is continuous up to the closure attains its maximum and minimum on the\nboundary, so two of them agreeing on the boundary agree everywhere inside. In\ntwo dimensions they are locally real parts of holomorphic functions, and\nglobally so on simply connected domains, which is a convenient source of\nexamples. The real part of , for instance, gives the harmonic\ncubic\n\n-- Verify that the real part of z^3 has zero Laplacian.\n#cas [laplacian x, y] (x ^ 3 - 3 * x * y ^ 2)\n\n\nwhose Laplacian the verified computer algebra system certifies to vanish:\n\n#cas [laplacian x, y]\nDomain: (x, y) ∈ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar ℝ): (x ^ 3 - 3 * x * y ^ 2)\nOutput (scalar ℝ): 0\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThis repeats the harmonic-polynomial check from Foundations at one higher\ndegree. The checked residual makes the cubic a candidate for a Dirichlet\nproblem with its own boundary values. Uniqueness, proved by the maximum\nprinciple below, is what identifies that candidate with any other solution\nhaving those values. We use both arguments because differentiation verifies\nthe formula, while uniqueness explains why the boundary data determine it.\n\nFor a forced example, take the interval  and solve\n with . Integrating twice gives\n\n\n\nThe solution is positive inside the interval. This checks the sign convention:\npositive forcing for  produces a concave solution in one dimension.\nFor , integration by parts gives\n, the smooth-test identity in\nthe weak formulation.\nThe zero endpoint values specify the homogeneous Dirichlet boundary class.\n\nThe two approaches to the general problem use different information about\nthe equation.\n\nThe classical route writes down explicit integral formulas, the fundamental\nsolution, Green's functions, Poisson kernels, and studies their pointwise\nbehavior. On the ball, the Poisson integral of a continuous boundary datum\n is the harmonic function extending continuously to those boundary values.\nThis gives concrete representations on model domains, and it leads to\nmean-value formulas, maximum principles, and interior derivative estimates.\n\nThe variational route starts with the weak equation. Rewrite\n as a bilinear form\n on  and ask only\nthat the form be bounded and coercive. Lax–Milgram then hands back a unique\nweak solution for every bounded linear load, and on a bounded domain an \nforcing is such a load. Bounded measurable principal coefficients suffice\nunder uniform ellipticity. Lower-order terms can destroy coercivity, so that\nLax–Milgram does not apply directly. The Fredholm alternative then describes\nsolvability: a load must be orthogonal to the adjoint kernel, and when the\nhomogeneous and adjoint kernels are both trivial one recovers unique solvability\nfor every load, just as in finite-dimensional linear algebra.\n\nGreen and Poisson kernels expose pointwise structure on model domains. The\nvariational theory instead gives a Sobolev element satisfying an integral\nidentity. Regularity theory is the\nbridge: a weak solution picks up extra Sobolev derivatives whenever the\ncoefficients, forcing, and boundary allow it, and with enough derivatives a\nSobolev embedding hands back a classical representative. We formalize the\n elliptic bootstrap at every finite Sobolev order under the chart,\ncoefficient, and forcing hypotheses for that order. Smooth charts and coherent\ndata at all orders allow Sobolev–Morrey reconstruction to produce one\nrepresentative smooth on  for the principal equation.\n\nThe classical formulas follow Chapter 2 of ,\nand the weak theory follows Chapter 6. For further classical estimates see\n; for Lax–Milgram, Fredholm theory,\nand spectral analysis see .\n\nTo recover the mean value, solve the ball Poisson problem with the boundary\nvalues of the given harmonic function. Uniqueness identifies the constructed\nsolution with that function throughout the ball. At the center, the Poisson\nkernel is constant on the sphere, so its weighted boundary integral becomes\nthe spherical average. Green's representation then writes a solution\nin terms of boundary data by correcting  with a domain-dependent\nharmonic function. This follows Evans's §2.2.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations","header":"5.1. Equilibrium, representation, and coercivity","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Equilibrium___-representation___-and-coercivity"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Higher-Regularity-and-Compatibility":{"contents":"Evans §7.2.3 proves higher regularity for the autonomous second-order\nhyperbolic Dirichlet problem. We split his argument into compatibility traces,\ndifferentiated Galerkin chains, weak-limit identification, and elliptic\nlifting, which shows which initial traces and spatial estimates each\nadditional derivative needs.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Weak Waves and Evolution","header":"7.3.3. Higher Regularity and Compatibility","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Higher-Regularity-and-Compatibility"},"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Sources":{"contents":" is the principal reference because it connects\nthe core equation families to the functional analysis needed to study them.\nWe contacted Evans about following his book, and he was excited about the project.\nResults that track it carry source locators, which refer to the AMS second\nedition (2010). The source notes record the contents of that edition and\nthe pages of selected passages. The explanations here\nare written for this project, not copied from the book.\n\nThe time-space arguments above draw on \nfor compactness and  for recovery of\ntime continuity.  supplies the context\nfor absolute continuity on lines and Sobolev composition. The abstract\nexistence methods lead to ,\n, , and\n for fixed points, monotonicity, and nonlinear\nvariational arguments.  is the\nreference for viscosity solutions.\n\nFor fluids, the choice of solution class is particularly important.\n is the historical\nsource for global weak Navier–Stokes solutions;\n studies partial regularity\nof suitable weak solutions.  and\n give interior and endpoint\nregularity criteria.  and\n develop the vorticity and functional\nframeworks,  the axisymmetric\nsetting, and  the nearly self-similar\nBoussinesq and Euler analysis. Fefferman's official description fixes the\nClay problem under discussion, and Wiedemann is the source for weak\nincompressible Euler solutions.\n\nThe computation chapter gives the references behind its algorithms: Moore\nand Neumaier on interval analysis, Berz and Makino on Taylor models, Risch\nand Bronstein on symbolic integration, and Sturm's theorem for real-root\ncertificates.\n\nBlowup for the De Gregorio model follows a different kind of singularity:\nvorticity grows while its physical support expands along the real line. Its\nformalization, the real-line  conclusion of Chen–Hou–Huang's Theorem 1,\nbuilds on this library's De Gregorio equation in the companion repository\nBlowupZoo.\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Introduction","header":"1.6. Sources","id":"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Sources"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Second-Variations-and-Dirichlet-Energy--Nonlinear-integral-functionals":{"contents":"For , the second variation has gradient,\nfield, and mixed terms. The gradient term can be isolated by variations\nthat oscillate rapidly in a fixed direction. This tests the matrix\nHessian only on rank-one matrices, the matrices produced by differentiating\none scalar oscillation times one vector amplitude.\n\nLet  be bounded and open and\n. Assume  is jointly\n for , with its values and first two derivatives\nextending continuously to .\nIf  has a local minimum at zero for a globally\n variation , thenIf this local-minimum condition holds for every smooth compactly supported\nvector variation in , then at every ,The domain needs no boundary regularity. The pointwise condition tests the\nmatrix Hessian on rank-one directions only, and it is necessary for a minimum\nbut not sufficient.\n\nDifferentiate  twice under the integral using the continuous\nsecond jet. The scalar second-derivative test gives the integral inequality.\nNext fix a smooth cutoff  supported in  and use the pair\n and\n.\nAdding their second variations cancels the mixed oscillatory terms and\ngives  for every real , whereHence . If the continuous rank-one Hessian were negative at an\ninterior point, a nonzero cutoff in a neighborhood where it stays negative\nwould give . This local contradiction proves the pointwise condition.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009Second Variations and Dirichlet Energy","header":"9.3.2.2. Nonlinear integral functionals","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Second-Variations-and-Dirichlet-Energy--Nonlinear-integral-functionals"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Traveling-Waves-and-Similarity--Dilation-instead-of-translation":{"contents":"Self-similarity replaces translation by dilation. Where a traveling wave is\ninvariant under , a self-similar field is invariant\nup to the amplitude factor  under the scaling\n, and its profile  depends\nonly on the invariant combination .\n\nThe forward self-similar ansatz isThe function is total in Lean; analytic theorems state domains such as\n explicitly.\n\nEvery similarity reduction rests on one time derivative. The amplitude\n contributes , and the moving coordinate\n has velocity , contributing\n. The combination  is the\ninfinitesimal generator of the scaling, and it is what a model equation later\nsets equal to the spatial terms.\n\nThe time derivative of a scaled profile splits into the derivative of its\namplitude and the derivative of its moving similarity coordinate. In\nparticular, for positive time the forward ansatz satisfies the generator\nidentityThe theorem is vector-valued and assumes differentiability only at the\nsimilarity point used in the calculation.\n\nDifferentiate the amplitude  and the similarity coordinate\n separately. The power rule gives\n and\n. The product and Fréchet chain rules then give\n, which is the stated\ngenerator formula.\n\nSpatial derivatives are simpler, because at fixed  the dilation\n is affine with derivative , and the amplitude\n passes through as a constant. For the forward ansatz,\n and ; these factors account for the powers\nof  in a reduced profile equation.\n\nCentered dilation contributes one spatial-scale factor to a gradient or\ndivergence and two factors to a Laplacian. Thus a scalar ansatz with\namplitude  and spatial scale  obeysThe gradient identity requires differentiability of  at ; the\ndivergence identity requires differentiability of each vector-field component\nthere. The Laplacian identity holds without assuming .\nNo time regularity of  or  is needed for these fixed-time identities.\n\nAt fixed time, the map  has derivative\n. The Euclidean chain rule therefore contributes one factor of\n to each first spatial derivative and leaves the scalar amplitude\n unchanged. For the Laplacian, translation commutes with each partial\nderivative and the dilation contributes , for any profile; the divergence identity\nis the same coordinate calculation componentwise.\n\nThe forward and backward ansatzes prescribe their scales as fixed powers of\ntime. Dynamically rescaled formulations, such as the Chen–Hou treatment of\nthe Boussinesq equations, leave the amplitude , the spatial scale\n, and the physical time  as unknown functions of a new\ntime , to be fixed by normalization conditions rather than by\nexponents.\n\nFor time-dependent amplitude , spatial scale , physical\ntime , and a field , the dynamic pullback isThe construction is purely kinematic: no PDE and no law for the three scale\nfunctions enters.\n\nWith three time-dependent scales, the  derivative of the pullback has\nthree contributions: the amplitude term , the physical-time term\n, and the moving-coordinate term . The\nlast is where the dilation velocity  enters, and it is what produces\nthe  advection terms of a rescaled equation.\n\nAssume the three scale functions are differentiable at  and  is\njointly differentiable at . The time derivative\nsplits into amplitude, physical-time, and moving-coordinate terms:The companion spatial theorem states that advection by  contributes\n. These two identities are the chain rules behind the\ndynamically rescaled Boussinesq equations.\n\nDifferentiate the scalar amplitude and the space-time composition separately.\nThe derivative of  is ; applying\nthe Fréchet chain rule and then the product rule gives the displayed formula.\nAt fixed time, differentiating  in the direction \ngives , which is  by linearity.\n\nThe heat kernel is the model forward self-similar field, with amplitude\nexponent  and spatial exponent : the exponent\n depends on  alone, and the prefactor\n is the amplitude. The scaling law is the finite form of\nthat invariance, stated directly for the Lean kernel rather than derived\nfrom the chain rules.\n\nFor  and , the Euclidean heat kernel obeys the exact parabolic\nscaling lawPositivity excludes the totalized nonpositive-time branch and removes every\nambiguity in the real powers.\n\nInsert  and  into\n. The quotient in the\nexponent is unchanged because both  and the time denominator gain\n. Positivity of  lets the prefactor simplify as\n, yielding the stated scaling law.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Traveling Waves and Similarity","header":"9.2.1.4.2. Dilation instead of translation","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Traveling-Waves-and-Similarity--Dilation-instead-of-translation"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Traveling-Waves-and-Similarity--Scaling-toward-a-finite-singular-time":{"contents":"Blow-up profiles use the time remaining to a singular time. Reverse time by\n and replace the forward amplitude exponent  by\n, so that the amplitude is . In the derivative,\nthe amplitude term keeps its sign because the exponent and the time direction\nboth flip, while the coordinate term flips once, so the generator is\n rather than .\n\nFor , the backward scale  is positive, and it\ndiverges as  when . If the profile is differentiable\nat , differentiating the backward self-similar field\nproduces the common factor  and the\nscaling generator . This is the kinematic\nidentity used before a model-specific profile equation is imposed.\n\nSet . Then  as  for\n. Differentiating  uses\n: the two minus signs turn the amplitude contribution positive, and\nthe similarity-coordinate derivative contributes\n. Both terms contain the common factor\n.\n\nThe generator identity concerns the reduced equation. Whether the backward\nfield is singular at  is a separate, norm-theoretic question:\nsince the dilation is onto, the spatial supremum of the field at time \nis  times the supremum of the profile. If the profile takes a nonzero\nvalue, divergence of  forces divergence of the supremum norm,\nwhatever the spatial exponent does.\n\nLet  be a scaled profile ansatz on a\nreal vector space  with values in a normed space . At every time\nwith , the extended spatial supremum of  is the amplitude\ntimes that of the profile,including the case of an unbounded profile. In that case both sides are\n when , and both are zero when . If along\na filter of times  eventually and\n at one fixed similarity coordinate ,\nthen  along that filter. In particular, the backward\nself-similar field\n with\n and a profile taking at least one nonzero value satisfieswith no boundedness assumption on the profile. If the profile is bounded,\nthen the spatial supremum is also finite for every , so the field\nsatisfies SpatialSupNormBlowsUpAt at . The spatial exponent\n is unrestricted: it moves the sampled point but not the amplitude.\nThe first two statements need only a norm and a compatible real scaling on\n; the backward-field statements take  to be a real normed space.\n\nFor fixed  with , the centered dilation\n is onto , so the supremum of\n over  equals the supremum of\n over , and the amplitude factors out of the norm\nand then out of the supremum. For the sampled growth statement, follow the\nmoving point , where the ansatz equals ;\ndivergence of its norm forces divergence of the supremum. For the backward\nfield, the amplitude  is positive for  and tends to\n as  when , so\n diverges at any\ncoordinate with . A global profile bound \ngives  for\neach , which supplies the finite-slice condition.\n\nNot every similarity solution decays or concentrates. The Burgers\nrarefaction  has amplitude exponent  and spatial exponent\n, so its amplitude neither grows nor shrinks, and the profile\n is the linear function that makes  vanish\nidentically inside the fan.\n\nOn positive time, the smooth interior Burgers rarefactionis the similarity profile  with . The positive-time\ndomain is explicit because the profile is singular at .\n\nChoose amplitude exponent , spatial exponent , center\n, and profile . The forward ansatz becomes\n whenever , exactly the rarefaction interior.\n\nIdentifying  as a similarity profile is a statement about its form.\nThat it solves Burgers is a separate statement, and the conservation form\n is the one relevant to weak solutions;\nintegrating the pointwise residual over a subdomain of positive time is the\nfirst step toward the integral identities a weak solution must satisfy.\n\nFor every subdomain of positive spacetime, the conservation-law residualof the smooth rarefaction interior integrates to zero. The theorem combines\nthe ordinary similarity statement with a CAS-checked pointwise residual.\n\nOn , differentiate  to obtain\n and\n. The verified symbolic\ncalculation proves these terms cancel pointwise. Integrating the zero residual\nover any subdomain of positive spacetime gives zero.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Traveling Waves and Similarity","header":"9.2.1.4.4. Scaling toward a finite singular time","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Traveling-Waves-and-Similarity--Scaling-toward-a-finite-singular-time"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Verified-Exact-Symbolic-Computation":{"contents":"An exact residual proof often ends with a cancellation. To establish\n on a domain , we can normalize\n to zero and prove that  throughout\n. Polynomial arithmetic can settle the first question; a sign,\ninterval, or root-exclusion argument can settle the second. The domain\ndetermines where we may use the resulting equality.\n\nThe normalization and polynomial-root certificates of the exact core cover\nconditional cancellation, real powers and exponentials, exact polynomial\ndivision, and Sturm chains. The calculus bridges use such identities to\njustify derivatives and antiderivatives.\n\n\n\nThe core expression type is an inductive syntax tree indexed by its variable\ntype. It contains rational constants, arithmetic operations, natural powers,\nreal powers with an arbitrary real exponent (Mathlib's Real.rpow),\ntrigonometric functions, exponential and logarithmic functions, square roots,\nand the inverse tangent, sine, and cosine. Evaluation uses Lean's total\nreal functions. A separate validity predicate records sufficient conditions\nfor their analytic rules: a nonzero denominator or logarithm argument, a\npositive square-root argument or power base, and an inverse sine or cosine\nargument different from . These conditions are needed by the\ncalculus bridges; an equality about total values need not require them.Schematically, the reflected language and its semantics areHere , the exponent  of  is a natural\nnumber while  in  is an arbitrary expression, and rational\nliterals with zero denominator are rejected by the well-formedness layer.\n\nIf the executable normalizer reduces an expression to literal zero, evaluating\nthe original expression in any real environment gives zero. The computation\ntherefore proposes a normal form, while this theorem is the kernel-checked\nsemantic justification.\n\nThe normalizer is accompanied by an invariant equating evaluation of each\nintermediate form with evaluation of the input. Specializing that invariant\nto a returned literal zero identifies the original value with the evaluation\nof zero, hence with zero in every environment.\n\nRational cancellation and other conditional rewrites return the nonzero\nconditions they require. Under those conditions, reduction to zero again\nimplies equality of the original real expression.If normalization returns a zero expression together with side conditions\n, then\n\nThe side-aware route proves evaluation preservation conditionally on every\nemitted nonzero or branch requirement. Once SideHolds supplies those\nrequirements, the route's final zero expression evaluates to zero, so the\noriginal expression does as well.\n\nEvery checked symbolic transformation is recorded by one route type. A\nSideClaimRoute stores the source and target expressions, the list of\nexpressions that must not vanish, and a proof that the target and source\nevaluate equally wherever that list holds; a positivity requirement is encoded\nas the nonvanishing of a square root. An unconditional transformation is the\nsame object with an empty list of assumptions.For a route with source , target , and assumption list ,\n\nThe side-conditioned pipeline contains a pass for the algebra of real powers\nand of the exponential, which it treats as the power of . Inside\na product or quotient all powers of one base collect into a single power,\nnested powers flatten, powers of products distribute, exponents are put into\na polynomial normal form, and a literal exponent is read back as a natural\npower, a reciprocal, or a square root. Each rewrite that Mathlib states for a\npositive base records the positivity of that base as a condition unless the\nbase is structurally positive (a positive numeral, , , an\nexponential, or a product of these); rewrites valid for the total\nReal.rpow, and the exponential identities, record nothing.Writing  for the output of the pass on ,\n\nEach local rewrite uses the corresponding identity for real powers, square\nroots, or exponentials under its recorded positivity conditions. The\nbottom-up traversal retains every condition from the rewrites it applies. Induction over\nthe traversal composes the local evaluation equalities into the equality for\nthe whole tree; when the expression contains no power atom the pass returns it\nunchanged.\n\nExact interval evaluation encloses the value of a sparse rational polynomial\nat every real point of a rational panel. The root checks first try the\ncentred form, the exact Taylor shift of the polynomial to the midpoint of the\npanel bounded term by term over its radius, and use termwise evaluation when\nthe centred interval does not exclude zero. This theorem is the semantic basis\nfor both root exclusion and derivative-sign checks.\n\nTermwise evaluation encloses each monomial with interval multiplication and\nnatural powers and combines the terms with interval addition. The basic\ninterval operations preserve membership, so induction over the sparse term\nlist shows that the real polynomial value lies between the computed rational\nendpoints. For the centred form, write  with the\nexact shifted coefficients , where  is the midpoint and  the\nradius of the panel. Since , an even power  lies in\n and an odd one in , so every term lies in a rational\ninterval and their sum encloses . The enclosure returns one of these\ntwo sound intervals.\n\nIf checked interval evaluation excludes zero, the polynomial is nonzero\nthroughout the panel. The result can be used directly as a side condition for\nan ordinary real-expression theorem.\n\nSound interval evaluation places every  in the computed range.\nThe Boolean root-free test proves that zero is outside that range. Therefore\nno enclosed value can equal zero.\n\nFor example, on , interval arithmetic gives\n. This one enclosure proves that the denominator\n never vanishes there and also gives\n. The nonvanishing fact justifies an exact\ncancellation; the two numerical endpoints control the size of the\nreciprocal. Exact reasoning and CEGA draw on the same domain information.\n\nEndpoint values satisfying , or the reversed\ninequalities, give a root by the intermediate value theorem. A checked\nstrict derivative sign makes the polynomial strictly monotone in the\ncorresponding direction. Together they prove that the rational bracket\ncontains exactly one real root, possibly at an endpoint.For every valid bracket certificate,\n\nContinuity and the weak endpoint inequalities give existence by the intermediate\nvalue theorem. The derivative enclosure has one strict sign on the whole\npanel, so the mean value theorem makes  strictly monotone there. A\nstrictly monotone function has at most one zero, proving uniqueness.\n\nA root atlas combines root-free and uniquely rooted panels with a proved\ncover. Every real root in the outer domain therefore belongs to one of the\natlas's isolated root regions.If  is an atlas over , its projection isFor a global atlas the premise  is removed by its outer-root bound.\n\nTake a root in the covered domain and choose an atlas panel containing it.\nA root-free panel is impossible because its certificate excludes zero.\nConsequently the chosen panel is one of the root regions, which proves the\ncovering conclusion. The global version first uses the outer bound to place\nthe root in the covered domain.\n\nA checked rational Cauchy certificate gives an explicit outer interval\ncontaining every real root. Its exact monic decomposition is verified before\nthe resulting coefficient bound is used.For its derived rational bound ,\n\nUse the checked monic decomposition to rewrite a hypothetical root equation.\nIf  were at least the computed coefficient bound, the leading term would\nstrictly dominate the sum of all lower-degree terms, contradicting that their\nsum is zero. Hence every root lies inside .\n\nUnderstanding a root certificate.\n\nThe isolated root need not be rational. A root atlas identifies it by a\nrational interval and a uniqueness theorem, which is often exactly what a PDE\nside-condition proof needs. The covering and uniqueness theorems of the\nRootAtlas and GlobalRootAtlas interfaces can discharge nonvanishing,\ndomain, and monotonicity side conditions in later symbolic or PDE arguments.\nThose theorems have the same form whether an atlas was assembled from\ninterval exclusion, Sturm subdivision, or both.\n\nThe Cauchy root bound in detail.\n\nFor a polynomial with rational coefficients, write it in monic form\n\n\n\nwhere  is the leading scalar. The checked bound is\n\n\n\nA CauchyBoundCertificate stores only the decomposition fields (scale ,\ndegree , and the lower-degree tail). The bound  is computed from that\ntail. An independent checker\nvalidates:\n\n1. the scale is nonzero;2. every exponent in the tail is strictly below ;3. the sparse identity  holds after normalization.\n\nWhen , the checked tail is empty and , so there are no\nroots. For , the soundness proof (abs_lt_bound) proceeds by\ncontradiction. If  and , dividing by  gives\n. Since , each lower-degree term\nsatisfies . Hence\n\n\n\nDividing by the positive factor  gives\n, a contradiction. The Lean proof implements this\nleading-term estimate with exact sparse evaluation and cancellation by the\nchecked nonzero scale.\n\nExample: isolating roots of .\n\nConsider the polynomial . A hand calculation shows the\ncertificate data; the generator may choose different split points.\n\n1. Cauchy bound. In monic form the tail is , whose coefficient\n   absolute values sum to . The checked bound is ,\n   so all roots lie in .2. Square-free reduction. The gcd of  and  is , so\n   p is already square-free and its radical is p itself.3. Sturm chain construction.* ,* ,* Euclidean division gives\n    , so\n    , a nonzero constant.The terminal polynomial  has zero derivative and nonzero value,\n  hence is globally nonzero (proved using the mean value theorem).4. Variation counting. At , the signs are , giving\n   two sign changes. At , they are , giving none. The\n   drop  confirms that p has exactly two distinct real\n   roots.5. Bisection. At , the signs are , giving one sign\n   change. Thus  and . The intervals\n    and  each contain exactly one root.6. Atlas assembly. The global root atlas records the two isolated intervals,\n   together with the checked Sturm certificate that proves their root counts.\n\nThe Sturm chain algorithm.\n\nA Sturm chain for a polynomial  is a sequence  satisfying:\n\n\n\nwhere the last element  is a nonzero constant. This signed Euclidean\nremainder sequence is the ordinary Euclidean algorithm with the sign of each\nremainder reversed. The certificate checker (SturmCertificate.valid)\nvalidates every recurrence by exact polynomial arithmetic, independently of\nthe generator that proposed the chain. The variation count  is the\nnumber of sign changes in , zeros omitted;\nSturm's theorem turns its drop\nbetween two endpoints into a root count.\n\nThe subdivision certificate.\n\nA Sturm subdivision is a binary tree of rational intervals over a search\ninterval, such as the Cauchy-bounded : every leaf has Sturm count at\nmost one, and every split is at a rational point that is not a root. SymLean\nchecks such a tree when a caller supplies it and has no generator for it; the\nroot and sign commands use their own root-cover search and root-list checkers.\nLeaves whose Sturm count is one are the isolated root regions.\n\nSparse polynomial representation.\n\nThe polynomial subsystem uses Verified.Expr.Alg.MvPoly n, a raw list of\nrational coefficient–monomial pairs. A monomial is a function\n recording the exponent of each variable.\nThe type permits repeated monomials and zero coefficients. The executable\nnormalization combines terms, removes zeros, and orders the result without\nchanging the polynomial's value.\n\nInterpreting these lists as Mathlib polynomials over  preserves\nevaluation. Importing a Mathlib multivariate polynomial and exporting it\nagain returns the original polynomial. In the other direction, different\nlists can represent the same\npolynomial, so a raw list need not equal its own round trip.\n\nKey operations include:\n\n* Euclidean division: exact quotient and remainder satisfying\n   for univariate polynomials.* Square-free factorization (squareFreeFactors?): a Yun-style candidate\n  generator proposes factors indexed by intended multiplicity. Its output is a\n  proposal; the semantic contract belongs to the radical certificate built\n  from it.* Rational factors (RationalFactorization.split): linear and quadratic\n  factors are kept; x is split off by exact division while it divides a\n  factor of degree three or more; any other factor of degree three or more is\n  split only by a factor provider, Hex's factorizer through the optional\n  CASHex, whose proper factors are accepted when their product is that\n  factor, and otherwise stays whole. Rational integration uses the list as a\n  proposal and checks its final primitive by differentiation.* Exact evaluation (evalRat): evaluates at rational points with exact\n  arithmetic, used for sign determination in Sturm chains.* Interval evaluation (evalInterval): rigorous enclosure of the\n  polynomial's range on a rational panel.\n\nAn exact quotient-and-remainder candidate is accepted only after sparse\npolynomial normalization proves the corresponding identity. The checked\nidentity is then projected to Mathlib's Polynomial ℚ.Every accepted certificate proves the polynomial identityin Polynomial ℚ, including the exact quotient and remainder supplied by the\ncandidate.\n\nTranslate the candidate quotient and remainder into the sparse normal form and\nnormalize . Acceptance means this difference has zero normal form.\nNormalization soundness gives the polynomial identity over the rationals,\nwhich is then transported to Mathlib's polynomial representation.\n\nA radical certificate proves that a proposed square-free reduction has exactly\nthe same real zero set as the original polynomial and has nonzero derivative\nat every root. The executable Yun-style generator returns only a certificate that\npasses the independent check of two divisibility identities and a Bézout identity.Writing the checked radical as ,\n\nThe checked division identities certify the gcd decomposition used by the\nsquare-free reduction. They show that removing repeated factors preserves\nexactly the zero set. Coprimality of the reduced polynomial and its derivative\nthen rules out a point where both vanish, so every remaining root is simple.\n\nAn accepted Sturm certificate begins with the requested polynomial and its\nformal derivative, satisfies every signed Euclidean recurrence exactly, and\nends in a polynomial that is nonzero on the real line. These are structural\nfacts about the checked chain, before any root-counting theorem is applied.For a chain , validation establishes\n\nThe independent checker verifies the first two entries and every signed\nquotient-remainder identity using exact polynomial normalization. At the\nterminal entry it checks a zero formal derivative and a nonzero value at zero.\nThe mean value theorem makes that polynomial constant, hence nowhere zero.\nCollecting these checked equalities yields the defining Sturm-chain\nconditions.\n\nFor an accepted chain and ordered rational endpoints that are not roots, the\ndrop in endpoint sign variations equals the number of distinct real roots in\nthe intervening open interval. This is the analytic Sturm theorem that gives\nthe executable variation count its mathematical meaning.\n\nAlong an interval containing no endpoint root, sign variation is locally\nconstant except when crossing a root of . The signed Euclidean recurrence\nshows that noninitial chain entries cannot create a net jump, while each\ndistinct root of  decreases the variation by one. Summing the jumps from\n to  gives the root count.\n\nA signed Euclidean chain is proposed outside the checker and accepted only\nwhen the independent structural checker SturmCertificate.valid accepts\nevery initial, recurrence, degree, and terminal condition. SymLean has no\nchain generator: a caller supplies the chain.\n\nSturm counts certify unique-root intervals and exact root counts for a checked\nsubdivision tree. An accepted finite subdivision therefore projects to the\nsame root-atlas interface used by interval and monotonicity certificates.For each accepted node  with interval ,and every leaf with count one projects to an\n certificate.\n\nApply the Sturm count theorem at every node. A node of count zero is root-free;\na node of count one contains a root and cannot contain two distinct roots.\nFor a split node, additivity of root counts over its adjacent children\npreserves the parent's exact count. Structural induction over the accepted\nsubdivision tree therefore produces the root atlas.\n\nThe isolation certificates combine square-free reduction, a Sturm\nsubdivision, transport back to the original zero set, and a checked rational\nCauchy outer bound. GlobalPolynomialIsolationCertificate.valid checks every\nstage, and the noncomputable toGlobalRootAtlas projection then yields a\nglobal atlas covering every distinct real root; a nonzero constant has an\nempty atlas. SymLean checks these certificates when a caller supplies them and\nhas no generator for them.The final global contract iswith each root-bearing region carrying a unique-root theorem.\n\nScope of the exact core.\n\nThe decision procedures of the exact core work inside its reflected real\nlanguage, with rational polynomial coefficients. A bounded search that returns\nnone has found no supported certificate within its budget; the statement\nitself may still be true.\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Exact Symbolic Calculus","header":"10.6.1. Verified Exact Symbolic Computation","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Verified-Exact-Symbolic-Computation"}});